Packages can be marked as existing in a particular directory.

First, demonstrate that packages cannot try to occupy the same directory:

  $ cat >dune-project <<EOF
  > (lang dune 3.21)
  > (package
  >  (name foo)
  >  (dir foo))
  > (package
  >  (name bar)
  >  (dir foo))
  > EOF

  $ dune build
  File "dune-project", line 7, characters 6-9:
  7 |  (dir foo))
            ^^^
  Error: package foo is already defined in "foo"
  [1]

  $ cat >dune-project <<EOF
  > (lang dune 3.21)
  > (package
  >  (name foo)
  >  (dir foo))
  > (package
  >  (name bar))
  > EOF

  $ mkdir foo
  $ cat >foo/dune <<EOF
  > (rule (with-stdout-to x.txt (echo foo bar)))
  > EOF

  $ dune build foo/x.txt

This should error because foo/x.txt is only visible to package foo

  $ dune build --only-packages bar foo/x.txt
  Error: Don't know how to build foo/x.txt
  [1]

We can try to define a rule that belongs to "foo" elsewhere, but it should not
work:

  $ mkdir bar
  $ cat >bar/dune <<EOF
  > (executable
  >  (public_name bar)
  >  (package foo))
  > EOF
  $ touch bar/bar.ml

  $ dune build @check
  File "bar/dune", line 3, characters 10-13:
  3 |  (package foo))
                ^^^
  Error: Package foo may not be defined here
  That package must exist in foo
  [1]

  $ rm -rf bar/

It should also be impossible to add non foo stanzas to foo/

  $ cat >foo/dune <<EOF
  > (executable
  >  (public_name foo)
  >  (package bar))
  > EOF
  $ touch foo/foo.ml

  $ dune build @check
  File "foo/dune", line 3, characters 10-13:
  3 |  (package bar))
                ^^^
  Error: Package bar may not be defined here
  The only package that can be defined in this directory is foo because the
  directory of this stanza is exclusive to this package
  [1]

Installed library metadata should be generated in the package's directory,
including deprecated package names. Packages without (dir) keep their metadata
at the project root. An empty package directory need not contain a dune file.

  $ mkdir metadata
  $ cd metadata
  $ cat >dune-project <<'EOF'
  > (lang dune 3.21)
  > (name metadata)
  > (generate_opam_files)
  > (package (name owned) (dir owned) (deprecated_package_names old-owned))
  > (package (name empty) (dir empty) (allow_empty))
  > (package (name root))
  > EOF
  $ mkdir owned empty lib
  $ cat >owned/dune <<'EOF'
  > (library (public_name owned))
  > (deprecated_library_name
  >  (old_public_name old-owned)
  >  (new_public_name owned))
  > EOF
  $ echo 'let value = 1' >owned/owned.ml
  $ echo '(library (name root_lib) (public_name root))' >lib/dune
  $ echo 'let value = 2' >lib/root_lib.ml
  $ cat >owned/META.owned.template <<'EOF'
  > # DUNE_GEN
  > custom = "owned"
  > EOF
  $ cat >META.root.template <<'EOF'
  > # DUNE_GEN
  > custom = "root"
  > EOF

  $ dune build @install
  $ find _build/default -name 'META.*' -o -name '*.dune-package' \
  >   | grep -v '\.template$' | sort
  _build/default/META.empty
  _build/default/META.old-owned
  _build/default/META.owned
  _build/default/META.root
  _build/default/empty.dune-package
  _build/default/old-owned.dune-package
  _build/default/owned.dune-package
  _build/default/root.dune-package
  $ grep '^custom' _build/install/default/lib/owned/META
  [1]
  $ grep '^(use_meta)' _build/install/default/lib/owned/dune-package
  [1]
  $ grep '^custom' _build/install/default/lib/root/META
  custom = "root"
  $ test -f _build/default/owned.install
  $ test -f owned.opam
  $ test ! -f _build/default/owned/owned.install
  $ test ! -f owned/owned.opam

Installation keeps the public metadata paths, including deprecated packages.
An external consumer can still link against the installed library.

  $ dune install --prefix "$PWD/_prefix" --display quiet
  $ find _prefix/lib -name META -o -name dune-package | sort
  _prefix/lib/empty/META
  _prefix/lib/empty/dune-package
  _prefix/lib/old-owned/META
  _prefix/lib/old-owned/dune-package
  _prefix/lib/owned/META
  _prefix/lib/owned/dune-package
  _prefix/lib/root/META
  _prefix/lib/root/dune-package
  $ echo 'let () = Printf.printf "%d\n" Owned.value' >consumer.ml
  $ OCAMLPATH="$PWD/_prefix/lib" ocamlfind ocamlc \
  >   -package old-owned -linkpkg consumer.ml -o consumer.exe
  $ ./consumer.exe
  1
  $ dune uninstall --prefix "$PWD/_prefix" --display quiet
  $ find _prefix -name META -o -name dune-package

Templates generated by rules should also be looked up in the package directory.

  $ rm owned/META.owned.template
  $ cat >>owned/dune <<'EOF'
  > (rule
  >  (target META.owned.template)
  >  (action
  >   (write-file %{target} "# DUNE_GEN\ncustom = \"generated-owned\"\n")))
  > EOF
  $ dune build @install
  $ grep '^custom' _build/install/default/lib/owned/META
  [1]

Loading a package's metadata rules must not require generating rules for an
unrelated, invalid executable at the project root.

  $ cat >dune <<'EOF'
  > (executable
  >  (name unavailable)
  >  (modules missing))
  > EOF
  $ touch unavailable.ml
  $ dune build _build/install/default/lib/owned/META \
  >   _build/install/default/lib/owned/dune-package
  File "dune", line 3, characters 10-17:
  3 |  (modules missing))
                ^^^^^^^
  Error: Module Missing doesn't exist.
  [1]
